                               THE LEDGER



   “Las otras casas,” said Abuela. The other houses. “My mother wrote
to them. Then I did. Now we call, when the phone works.” She turned
one envelope over so I could read the return address. N. Byrne,
Rosnaree, Co. Meath. “Every place where the ground listens, there is a
family that listens back. They keep their places the way we keep the
stones. Nobody pays them. Nobody counts them.”
   “Do they know about this?” I meant the ledger, the bell, the speaker
across the street.
   “They know the bell.” She retied the string. “Everybody who keeps
a place knows the bell.”
   At the bottom of the trunk, in a side pocket sewn into the canvas,
was a thicker plastic bag. Inside, a stack of envelopes, cream-colored,
good paper, each with the iris in the corner.
   I looked at her.
   “Siete,” said Abuela. Seven. “Since January. Two a month, lately.”
She shrugged. “I don’t answer.”
   I took out the top one. It was typed, polite, on stationery so heavy it
felt like cloth. Aperture Civic · Caribbean Community Initiative. An offer to
purchase the property on this street, at a figure that would have made
my mother cry. Warm, patient language. We would so value your steward‐
ship of the neighborhood’s history. The most recent one was dated last
week. Under the offer, a postscript, typed on its own line:
   We understand your grandson may be visiting soon. We would be so glad
to meet him.
   It was signed in a small, neat hand. Your neighbor, Adrian Vale.
   I looked at the date again. Last week, before I’d booked a ticket. Be‐
fore I’d decided. Before my mother opened a can.
   “Abuela. How did he know I was coming?”
   “Ellos cuentan,” said my grandmother, as if it were a thing a person
could say about the weather.
   She took the letter from my hand. She slid it into the bag with the
others, and zipped it, and set it on top of the pages in the trunk, a new
volume, twenty-ninth in a very old book. Then she closed the lid and
sat on it, with her hands on her knees, and looked out of the window at
the speaker over the colmado door.



